Team,

Confirming the standing rule so we stop re-litigating this every time a new diagnostics engagement gets booked. For all diagnostics and instrumentation clients, we hold a flat 5% budget reserve regardless of spend level, to buffer against regulatory review delays. This applies to every regulated client on our books.

The 5% budget reserve is client-type driven, not spend driven. Whether the client is at $40K a quarter or $400K a quarter, the reserve line comes off the top at the flat 5% rate before any allocation to platforms or production. Accounts, please continue to seed this into the reserve column of every new SO for a regulated client at booking; do not wait for the campaign plan.

The rationale (for anyone new to the file): FDA advisory notices, FDA labeling and promotional-review holds, and state-level diagnostic advertising rulings can and do interrupt live campaigns with no notice. The flat 5% is what has historically kept us from having to claw budget back out of vendors mid-flight when a claim gets pulled or a piece has to be re-cleared. It is not a discount, it is not a contingency negotiation lever with the client, and it is not a line item we surface on the client-facing budget summary — it lives inside the managed-spend envelope.

If any of you see the diagnostics reserve being applied on a sliding scale in a SO or a proposal draft, please flag it back to me and to Marcus before the doc goes out. Regardless of spend, regardless of client size, regardless of quarter — flat 5%, every diagnostics and instrumentation client, no exceptions.

Thanks,
